// Cloudflare OAuth — "Connect Cloudflare" instead of pasting an API token.
//
// Plain server-side module (NO "use server") — imported by the OAuth routes, the
// server actions and the DNS helpers. Never bundled to the client; no token or
// secret is ever logged or returned to the browser.
//
// Nxeon is a server-side Next.js app, so we are a CONFIDENTIAL client: the
// Authorization Code flow with a client secret (client_secret_post), PLUS PKCE
// (S256) because Cloudflare advertises support for it and it costs us nothing.
// Cloudflare supports ONLY the authorization-code grant for third-party clients
// (no client-credentials / implicit / device), plus refresh_token for renewals.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PROTOCOL CONSTANTS — the one block to correct if Cloudflare changes anything.
//
// CONFIRMED from Cloudflare docs + their live OIDC discovery document
// (https://dash.cloudflare.com/.well-known/openid-configuration, fetched
// 2026-07-25):
//   * authorize  https://dash.cloudflare.com/oauth2/auth
//   * token      https://dash.cloudflare.com/oauth2/token
//   * revoke     https://dash.cloudflare.com/oauth2/revoke
//   * grant_types_supported includes "authorization_code" and "refresh_token"
//   * token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported includes client_secret_post/basic
//   * code_challenge_methods_supported includes "S256"
//   * "offline_access" is a supported scope (that's what yields a refresh token)
